        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          How to Apply Acrylic Sealer: Step-by-Step
        </h2>

        <p>
          Acrylic sealer application follows a consistent process whether you are working on a driveway, patio, or walkway, and the vast majority of application failures trace back to skipping or rushing one of these steps.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 1: Clean and Fully Dry the Surface</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Pressure wash the concrete at 2,500-3,000 PSI to remove dirt, oil, efflorescence, and any peeling remnants of a previous sealer. Degrease stained areas with an alkaline concrete cleaner before rinsing. Then wait — a minimum of 48 hours of dry weather is standard, longer in humid climates or on concrete that has not fully cured from a recent pour. Trapped moisture beneath an acrylic film is the leading cause of the whitish haze known as blushing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 2: Test for Existing Sealer and Compatibility</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Sprinkle water on the surface. If it beads up, an old sealer is likely still active and needs to be assessed for compatibility or stripped before recoating — applying acrylic over an incompatible existing coating (or over a peeling one) guarantees premature failure. Solvent-based acrylics can generally be stripped with xylene and a stiff brush; check compatibility with a small test patch before committing to the full surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 3: Apply Two Thin Coats, Not One Thick Coat</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Use a 3/8-inch nap roller for most driveway, patio, and walkway surfaces, working in manageable 50-75 square foot sections. Keep the roller damp rather than dripping, and maintain a wet edge to avoid visible lap marks. Allow the manufacturer's specified recoat window — typically 2-4 hours for solvent-based acrylics and slightly less for water-based — before applying the second coat. Two thin coats produce a smoother, better-bonded, longer-lasting film than one heavy application every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 4: Keep Traffic Off Until Fully Cured</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Foot traffic can typically resume 24 hours after the final coat, but full cure — the point at which the film reaches its maximum hardness and abrasion resistance — takes 5-7 days for most acrylic formulas. Keep vehicles off a freshly sealed driveway for at least 72 hours, longer in cool or humid weather, since hot tire pickup risk is highest before the film has fully hardened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between solvent-based and water-based acrylic sealers?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Solvent-based acrylics dissolve resin in a carrier like xylene, which penetrates concrete pores before evaporating and leaves a thicker, glossier film with stronger UV retention. Water-based acrylics suspend resin particles in water, which coalesce into a film as the water evaporates. Water-based formulas are lower odor, non-flammable, and easier to clean up, but they typically produce a thinner film with less dramatic gloss and require more frequent recoating on driveways.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What gloss level should I choose for an acrylic concrete sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              High-gloss wet-look acrylics measuring 80-plus gloss units deliver the most dramatic color enhancement and are popular on driveways and showcase patios, but they show dirt more easily and can be slippery when wet. Satin acrylics in the 40-60 range provide a more natural look with better everyday practicality. Flat or low-sheen acrylics protect with minimal visual change, best for surfaces where you want protection without altering the concrete's appearance.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does acrylic concrete sealer last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Solvent-based acrylic sealers typically last two to three years on patios and walkways and one to two years on driveways before recoating is needed. Water-based acrylics generally need recoating slightly more often, especially on surfaces exposed to vehicle traffic. UV exposure, traffic level, and climate all affect the actual lifespan, and the water droplet test is the simplest way to check: if water no longer beads on the surface, it is time to reseal.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will acrylic sealer make my concrete yellow?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Lower-quality acrylic sealers, especially budget solvent-based formulas without UV-stabilized resins, can yellow within 12 to 18 months of direct sun exposure. Premium acrylics formulated with non-yellowing UV-stable resin packages resist this for three to five years or more. Water-based acrylic emulsions generally resist yellowing even better than solvent-based formulas due to differences in the UV stabilizer chemistry used in each type.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is acrylic sealer slippery when wet?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              High-gloss acrylic sealers can become noticeably slippery when wet, which is a real safety concern on walkways, steps, and pool-adjacent patios. Satin and matte acrylic sheens are less slippery than high gloss. For any surface that gets wet regularly, mixing in a polymer bead or fine aluminum oxide non-slip additive before application is strongly recommended regardless of gloss level.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of acrylic sealer do I need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Two thin coats is the standard specification for most solvent-based acrylic sealers, applied two to four hours apart. Water-based acrylics often require two to three coats to reach a comparable dry film thickness because each coat deposits less resin than a solvent-based coat. Applying one thick coat instead of multiple thin coats is the most common application mistake and leads to bubbling, uneven gloss, and premature failure.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

